					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Useless trivia note... Rylden&#039;s reference to dihydrogen monoxide was not to show off that he could name it&#039;s &quot;proper covalent molecule&quot;.  It was a nod to a chain e-mail that circulated a few years ago warning people of the dangers of dihydrogen monoxide.  Yeah yeah, I shouldn&#039;t explain the joke.&nbsp; That&#039;s no fun...&nbsp; :banghead2: oh well...<br /><br />Snopes page about it:  <br />http://www.snopes.com/toxins/dhmo.htm<br />A student used it for his science fair in 1997, though the hoax was circulated on the internet before that time.  Some town came close to falling for it.  <br /><br />A google search turned up http://www.dhmo.org/ .  Someone&#039;s really gotten carried away and made an entire website about the dangers of dihydrogen monoxide.  Fear it!   :bandevil:<br /><br /></div>
