					<hr />
					<div style="margin: 0 5ex;">Ialin,<br /><br />You know some of the story behind myself and UoP. In particular was not really Migatol. In the months prior to my departure Migatol was never on, which was my biggest concern. He wasn&#039;t running the guild. It lacked leadership period. I was blasted for pushing the guild forward. As it was pointed out I had I guess what you would call a rogue raiding click. We would move on targets on off nights, and that made the non-hard raiders upset. One they felt left out and they felt we were moving too fast. Ironic that the VZ Conviction post was mentioned as that was the heart in the dagger for my so called push for the guild. Conviction had been hammering VZ early, before we could get enough numbers. UoP being casual could only mount a raid force at 7 EDT, Conviction would be ready by 5-6. So I stepped in to get UoP&#039;s foot in the door. I had this so called rogue core meet the day VZ was to spawn and be ready to go with a minimal force. I did not wait for everyone and got our first VZ kill and a limited number of flags. That upset those that could not log in to get the flag. That was just part of the story. Migatol was never on and made no attempt to play leadership. The majority of his raiding time post ToV was spent in a corner afk or not even on. Our first AHR kill was lead by my rogue faction while he was away at Fainfiare which also lead to a bitter taste in the officers mouth. Yes, I pushed them. But I got more accomplished in that short period than Migatol had. The guid was split due to this. 3 officers left due to some of this and then the &quot;core&quot; of UoP left with it. That is just part of the story, but in a nutshell Migatol &quot;was&quot; a great leader at one time, but at the end he caused more problems by his absence than he did prior to that.</div>
